From: Loong <galletzeynep481@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] net: httpd: redesign the recovery page

The page a user meets while their router is bricked was a bare heading and
an unstyled file input.  Rework it into something that reads as a finished
tool rather than a debug stub:

  - Chinese copy, since that is who this build is for, with the file names
    kept verbatim so they can be matched against the release assets.

  - Responsive layout and a manual light/dark switch.  Recovery often
    happens on a phone, plugged into the LAN port, with no other machine
    around.  The theme is a deliberate toggle rather than
    prefers-color-scheme; the choice is remembered in localStorage and
    applied from a <head> script, so it survives the page swap that follows
    an upload without flashing the wrong palette first.

  - An upload progress bar, driven by XMLHttpRequest.upload.onprogress.
    A 20 MB upload over 100BASE-TX takes long enough that a motionless page
    reads as a hang, and a second click posts again -- which the
    one-upload-at-a-time guard rejects without the user seeing why.  The
    form falls back to a plain submit if anything in the script throws, so
    a browser that dislikes the JS still flashes.

  - The bar reaching 100% is not the end: the board still has to scan a
    20 MB body for part boundaries.  That gap gets its own message rather
    than a full bar and nothing happening.

  - A summary of what is about to be written, built from the picked files,
    plus a sanity check on their names.  Three identical file inputs are
    easy to mix up and the consequences are not symmetric: a wrong file in
    the firmware slot leaves a board that still serves this page, while a
    wrong file in the BL2 slot lands at flash 0x800, the BootROM finds no
    FIP, and the only way back is serial xmodem.  The check warns rather
    than blocks -- names get renamed.

    That warning is repeated as a confirm() on submit, together with one
    for "rebuild UBI", and only ever for those two.  A blanket "are you
    sure" would be noise here: reaching this button already takes holding
    reset through a power cycle, a cable, a browser and a file picker, so
    nobody arrives by accident.  These two are different because they are
    the cases a second attempt cannot fix -- a bad firmware image still
    leaves a board that serves this page, while a misplaced BL2 or an
    erased ri volume does not.

  - The flashing page lists the write plan, in the order httpd_flash()
    actually runs it, labelled as a plan and paired with a line saying the
    page receives nothing during the write.  It is a plan and not progress:
    net_loop() has already returned by then and run_command() blocks, so
    there is no channel left to report on.  Nothing in the list is
    emphasised as "current" -- an emphasised row in a step list reads as
    the step being executed, which is precisely the wrong thing to suggest
    here.  "Rebuild UBI" carries a warning about its duration instead.  The
    browser knows what was picked and the response is a static string that
    does not, so the list is filled in after document.write() from the same
    script context -- U-Boot only carries the empty placeholder.  This is
    the one screen where a user is most likely to reach for the power
    switch, and an erase of 256 MiB with no explanation is exactly what
    makes them do it.

  - The panel LED legend moves into a table on the page itself.  It is the
    only status channel once the upload finishes -- the browser cannot show
    flashing progress, because by then U-Boot has left the network loop.

  - U_BOOT_VERSION in the footer, so a user reporting a problem can say
    which build they are looking at without a serial console, plus a
    WEB_VERSION badge next to the heading for the page itself.  The page
    and the U-Boot it ships in are versioned separately because they change
    for different reasons.

  - The "rebuild UBI" checkbox is now inside a warning box that spells out
    what it erases, and the whole bootloader section stays folded away, so
    the common case (flash firmware) is a single visible field.

Everything is inline: the CSP of a rescue page is "there is no network
beyond this device".  Both pages together add about 13 KB of rodata.

